 ## Changelog
+- 2026-10-13: FIX grass-side fringe flickering between bright and dim (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; `mesh::grass_overlay_tint_and_rail_quad` changed, the WGSL is covered by `chunk_shader_validates_and_legacy_is_forward`; video `Record_2026-10-13-16-08-56.mp4`):
+    - Symptom, measured on the video: the camera is still for frames ~28-60 and the frames are identical, so nothing flickers there; while it moves, the green fringe along the top of a grass block's side face is bright in frames 72, 73, 85, 87 and dark in 74-83, 84, 86, i.e. it flips from one frame to the next at the same view.
+    - Cause: the overlay (tile 38, grey fringe x grass tint = the dark one) was a second quad on the SAME plane and the SAME vertices as the face (tile 3, which has a bright untinted green fringe baked in), told apart only by a depth pull of 5e-7 (8 ulps) in `vs_main`. Two differently triangulated coplanar quads rasterise a few ulps apart, and a phone GPU interpolates depth with less precision than that at a grazing angle (the camera looking down a side face), so per pixel and per frame either quad won: the base's bright fringe showed through the overlay. UNVERIFIED on a device, but it is the only thing in the frame that decides this by depth, and it matches the toggling.
+    - Fix, no depth test left to lose: `mesh.rs` emits one quad per grass side (it carries the grass tint, the bottom and a snowy side stay white) and `fs_main` composites the overlay on tile 3 (`ouv` = tile 38, mip-0 alpha cut-out, colour from `atlas_sample_g` x tint, else the untinted base). `atlas_sample_g` is `atlas_sample` with the uv derivatives passed in, so the branch needs no uniform control flow. The depth pull in `vs_main` is gone. Cost: 4 quads fewer per grass block, 1 more texture read only on grass sides.
+    - ponytail: the outline's 0.005 offset is kept (its comment named the old 2 mm overlay); other coplanar pairs would flicker the same way (none known).
 - 2026-10-13: FIX lines along block edges + white flicker on textures (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; the clamp maths ran in `tools/atlas_bleed_check.py` on the real `terrain.rgba`, the WGSL is validated by the existing `chunk_shader_validates_and_legacy_is_forward`, which gained one assert; video `Record_2026-10-13-14-24-31.mp4`):
     - Root cause, one shared read in `gpu/pipeline.rs`: every block texture is a 16 px tile of one atlas, and the sampler is `Linear` (minified, i.e. distant or slanted faces, and everything at `RENDER_SCALE` 0.67) with `Linear` mips. A bilinear tap within half a texel of a tile edge reads the NEIGHBOURING tile, and the UV inset of 0.02 texel (`atlas::terrain_uv`) only covers mip 0, so at mip 1/2 a tap at the edge takes ~half of the next tile (`Repeat` also wraps tile 0 into tile 15 and the items rows). Measured in the script: up to 97/255 of colour from outside the tile (planks next to a transparent-black neighbour, dirt next to sand, ...), changing with the lod as the camera moves = lines at the block ends and a flicker. Transparent texels are black (`terrain.rgba`), the bright ones are light neighbours.
     - Fix: `atlas_sample` (WGSL) replaces both `textureSample(tex, ..)` calls (`fs_main`, `fs_water`): the UV is clamped into its own tile by half a texel of the coarsest mip the sampler will touch (`ceil(lod)`, `lod` from the same derivatives), then `textureSampleGrad`. Magnified (Nearest) it changes nothing; the flat-colour strip is read at mip 0 (before, its mips blended neighbouring flat colours on items and mobs). The script's worst edge error goes from 97 to 0.
